  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1116 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nWith the region solidly under a very warm airmass, as evidenced by\n850 mb temperatures in the upper teens, record to near-record\nhighs are expected to continue across the region Sunday and Monday\nahead of an approaching low pressure system. Although the windiest\nconditions are behind us, gusty winds are still possible today and\nless so on Monday as the pressure gradient becomes more baggy.\n\nLight rain still looks on track to arrive late Monday afternoon \nas transient shortwave and weak low pressure traverse the upper \nMississippi Valley. Current setup has a high PoP (50 to 90%) low \nQPF look to it, as the parent airmass doesn't look particularly \nmoist. As such, NBM probabilistic guidance only shows a 30 to \n70% chance for receiving 0.25\" of rain through Tuesday morning.\nThe best chance for the highest rainfall amounts are currently\nacross north-central Wisconsin (50-70% for 0.25\" or more) where\nthe best dynamics and moisture will coincide for the longest\nperiod of time. A rumble or two of thunder wouldn't be out of the\nquestion Monday evening as meager instability (~500 to 600 J/kg \nMUCAPE) builds along the front, though any convective activity \nwould remain elevated with little to no impacts. \n\nNext chances for widespread soaking precip arrive toward the end\nof next week as a deepening surface low and closed upper trough\ntrack from the central Plains into the Midwest. System currently\nhas a very winter-like appearance to it, especially with\npotential phasing scenario at play if we are to believe the more\naggressive LREF ensemble clusters. However, there is still a lot\nof variability this far out in the forecast period as there are\nsome model solutions that do not phase the system until it tracks \nfurther east, which would mean little to no precipitation for our \narea late next week. Whatever solution ends up panning out it does\nlook like next weekend will be a lot cooler as highs only reach\nthe 50s.",
